<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tây Bắc Autonomous Region</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1955">1955</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Thai-Meo Autonomous Region</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tây Bắc Autonomous Region was an autonomous region of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> covering the mountainous north-west, home to Tai, Hmong and other minority peoples. Created in 1955 as the Thái–Mèo Autonomous Region after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> Accords gave the area to North Vietnam, it was renamed Tây Bắc ('North-west') in 1962 and abolished in 1975, when its provinces came under direct central rule.</p></div>
